{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god","id":1045353,"title":"What it takes to be a god.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-02-07T13:42:38-08:00","id":1045353,"title":"What it takes to be a god.","content":"I was going to post this in the ask neall thread. but i realized that i wanted the opinions of everybody not just neall so i thought it's own topic would work better. i was wondering about what makes gods powerful. so i tried to think about the things all gods have in common, they're all powerful obviously, but what they do with the power is more important than the power itself. Set killed his brother in a much more larger than life way than any murder before him, an act of betrayal bigger than any , zeus won a battle agains the king of the universe aand became the king himsel, an act of battle and overthrowing bigger than any other.<br /> The question, would they still be gods if they hadnn't? If set decided he doesn't really care aabout his brother and just stayed in his lane minding his own bussines would he still be legend 12, would zeus be legend 12 if he chose to stay in his lane and not challenge kronos for fear of losing? would they still be powerful regardless?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4621,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","user":"Nicolas Milioni","date":". 02-07-2017, 05:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T13:45:30-08:00","id":1045355,"title":"","content":"My Onions...<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mixed_onions.jpg/220px-Mixed_onions.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T14:47:13-08:00","id":1045403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045355#post1045355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My Onions...<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mixed_onions.jpg/220px-Mixed_onions.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>i'm still forgetful as all hades <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T15:30:16-08:00","id":1045431,"title":"","content":"Lots and lots of chin-ups.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1675-weirdboyz","name":"Weirdboyz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"212"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T16:09:47-08:00","id":1045450,"title":"","content":"No serious answer?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T16:32:08-08:00","id":1045470,"title":"","content":"The answer is that there is no consistent, common feature that makes someone a God. It varies Pantheon to Pantheon. Scion is somewhat tricky in this regard since it sort of makes all Gods Gods, when a Theoi, a Teotl, and a Dia are all incredibly different beings who may not even recognize the others as being exactly like themselves. What makes them powerful also varies Pantheon to Pantheon, it's all a sort of mess.<br /> <br /> If they hadn't taken the actions they did in their Myths, they might or might not still be Gods. The Theoi would, since being a God is a species to them for example. If Tenjin of the Kami did not do what he did, if he was not as important, or influential as a mortal, he would not have been a God as he was originally a mortal who underwent Apotheosis.<br /> <br /> Would they still be Legend 12? Well, maybe? The way we know someone is Legend 12 tends to be from their power displayed in myths, or really crazy cult worship. So, if they didn't have any myths (as sometimes happens such as with some Hittite Deities) they might not be Legend 12 unless they are crazy important religiously. If Zeus never undertook any of the deeds he did mythically, but was worshiped exactly the same amount, he would probably still be Legend 12, really important dude. But if, say, Lugh had never taken any action, he probably wouldn't be Legend 12 since we have no record of him having any real cult worship in Ireland, not anything concrete anyways.<br /> <br /> It's... kind of complicated, the Legend 12 bit since it's purely a game-perspective thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 02-07-2017, 07:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T19:19:24-08:00","id":1045568,"title":"","content":"Power, the willingness to use it, and the recognition by <i>someone</i> that you exist and are worthy of worship/respect/notice. Being an all-powerful but anonymous being doesn't really make you a God, simply because you are unknown [the old &quot;tree falling in the forest&quot; argument]. &quot;Power&quot; can mean &quot;I am the God of Storms&quot;, or it can mean &quot;I am the Demigod of Art&quot; - it doesn't mean Creators or War Gods only. But you have to be recognized as a superior practitioner of your chosen/granted purview, and you have to be willing and able to do something with your power. That's the &quot;definition&quot; of a God/Goddess, IMHO.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T21:55:24-08:00","id":1045610,"title":"","content":"I think in another thread, Neall mentioned that in the universe of Scion, you don't really have to identify yourself as a God. It's something people know just by looking at you. Almost CERTAINLY not in a way that they could put their fingers on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T23:13:16-08:00","id":1045631,"title":"","content":"I remember Baba Yaga was mentioned a few times as an example of something that blurs the line regarding divinity. The threshold is usually at Legend 8 to Legend 9, and she's definitely crossed that. She's not a god, but she isn't <i>not</i> a god either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12689-creepyshutin","name":"CreepyShutIn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12689_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T05:06:27-08:00","id":1045688,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045568#post1045568\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Power, the willingness to use it, and the recognition by <i>someone</i> that you exist and are worthy of worship/respect/notice. Being an all-powerful but anonymous being doesn't really make you a God, simply because you are unknown [the old &quot;tree falling in the forest&quot; argument]. &quot;Power&quot; can mean &quot;I am the God of Storms&quot;, or it can mean &quot;I am the Demigod of Art&quot; - it doesn't mean Creators or War Gods only. But you have to be recognized as a superior practitioner of your chosen/granted purview, and you have to be willing and able to do something with your power. That's the &quot;definition&quot; of a God/Goddess, IMHO.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> When you get right down to it, the Gods are galactic leaders. Mortals worship them because they desire protection, guidance, or aid. Except while a mortal leader may be restricted to a specific nation, the Gods are placed in charge on a cosmic scale, and their authority doesn't recognize foreign borders. If the Gods straight up ignored the plights of mortals, well then, nobody would genuinely worship them. What would be the point of doing so if your prayers go unheard?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T06:44:42-08:00","id":1045717,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045470#post1045470\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The answer is that there is no consistent, common feature that makes someone a God. It varies Pantheon to Pantheon. Scion is somewhat tricky in this regard since it sort of makes all Gods Gods, when a Theoi, a Teotl, and a Dia are all incredibly different beings who may not even recognize the others as being exactly like themselves. What makes them powerful also varies Pantheon to Pantheon, it's all a sort of mess.<br /> <br /> If they hadn't taken the actions they did in their Myths, they might or might not still be Gods. The Theoi would, since being a God is a species to them for example. If Tenjin of the Kami did not do what he did, if he was not as important, or influential as a mortal, he would not have been a God as he was originally a mortal who underwent Apotheosis.<br /> <br /> Would they still be Legend 12? Well, maybe? The way we know someone is Legend 12 tends to be from their power displayed in myths, or really crazy cult worship. So, if they didn't have any myths (as sometimes happens such as with some Hittite Deities) they might not be Legend 12 unless they are crazy important religiously. If Zeus never undertook any of the deeds he did mythically, but was worshiped exactly the same amount, he would probably still be Legend 12, really important dude. But if, say, Lugh had never taken any action, he probably wouldn't be Legend 12 since we have no record of him having any real cult worship in Ireland, not anything concrete anyways.<br /> <br /> It's... kind of complicated, the Legend 12 bit since it's purely a game-perspective thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>when you think about it. The only thing gods from different pantheon have I common is power. otherwise everything is different. but where does that power came from?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T08:12:35-08:00","id":1045766,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045717#post1045717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">when you think about it. The only thing gods from different pantheon have I common is power. otherwise everything is different. but where does that power came from?</div> </div> </div> </div>Power is a pretty major defining source of Divinity, though, once again we see the Pantheons being really annoying and the source of their Power differs Pantheon to Pantheon. I can give a little breakdown of a collection of different Pantheons and how they... 'work' I guess?<ul><li>The <b>Tuatha</b> are presented as a Species, sort of, but their power is certainly not innate in their entity. Power is something that you have gotten, trained in essentially. You have the Tuatha described as great magicians, their knowledge of magic being something overwhelming and awesome. Similarly to this, Skills sort of exist in a semi-magical role. A great warrior knows a series of what appear to be semi-magical feats, such as The Salmon Leap or CuChulain cutting all of the hair off a man. The Tuatha's power is drawn through learned skills we can see. Also there is a great importance for Relics, magical objects. The Tuatha are rocking some of the most powerful Relics of the Pantheons, the Deva being greater, but they function at a far more massive sense of scale than the Irish.</li> <li>The <b>Theoi</b> and the <b>Shiunesh</b> (Hittites) both have innate inborn power. Ares is a War God, he is inherently a War God, not having any learned skills, or tools to provide him with this capacity. These Pantheons are sort of defined through their in-born power, and their very being sort of getting close to the concept. Ares is War, chaotic and violent. Tarhun is the Storms, booming, and dominant, that sort of thing. The Theoi and Shiunesh draw their power through their very being, they have power because they are Gods.</li> <li>The <b>Anunna</b> are an interesting bridging point between some ideas. The Gods are powerful, they are better than us, mighty and just an overwhelming awing force. However, they are given authority in specific areas, the Gods awarded Mes by one of their elder Deities, and these Mes are sort of like divine writs of authority over aspects of reality. Mes of prostitution, war, magic, fertility, animals, that sort of thing. The Mes can be given away, and stripped from Deities in various situations, showing that the Gods don't have any inherent power in themselves, but their power is drawn through these Mes.</li> <li>The <b>Shen</b> exist in a similar place as the Anunna, sometimes. Some entities, such as Nuwa, are inherently divine entities capable of great skills, such as the Theoi and the Shiunesh. However, at the same time their focus on mortals being uplifted to the status of Deity has power as something they can inherit from being a Deity. However, a lot of these entities, from what I know from the organization of the Shen, is that these Deified entities don't really have inborn power, past basic 'Deity' stuff. Their actual Purviews are drawn through their positions within the bureaucratic system which oversees the functioning of reality. When you become the Senior-Advisor to the Deputy Minister of the Yellow River, you probably have some influence over Water.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T10:04:16-08:00","id":1045839,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045717#post1045717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> when you think about it. The only thing gods from different pantheon have I common is power. otherwise everything is different. but where does that power came from?</div> </div> </div> </div>To continue Watcher's list, the <b>Deva</b> draw Power from Brahman, the Single Undivided Reality that is the basis for the World and all the Realms above, below, besides and within it. Through the practice of Yoga (literally 'addition' or 'joining') the Hindu Gods and their followers become One with That Which Is All (or, depending on exactly how you interpret it, realize that they <b>were</b> One all along), and the resulting Oneness is the source of all Power in Hinduism...basically, once you've realized that You <b>are</b> the Universe, causing, or becoming, a Miracle is as easy as blinking.<br /> <br /> According to popular interpretation, Brahma Vishnu and Shiva are either the closest to Unity with Brahman, or are the Three Original Manifestations of Brahman and this is why they are the Trimurti.<br /> <br /> A related concept is Shakti, the Sanskrit word for Power...meaning everything from the energy you use to move, to the electricity powering your toaster, to the cosmic dance of galaxies, to the Legendary miracles of the Gods. If Brahman IS all things, then Shakti is the Power to DO all things, if Brahman is All-Powerful, Shakti is All-Power, and the two are not separate things, being, rather, Passive and Active forms of the same thing. Shakti is embodied in the Goddesses, since in Hinduism the Active Dynamic Aspect of Existence is Female and the Inert Static Aspect is Male, in a twist on the more popular Yin Yang system.<br /> <br /> And just as Brahma Vishnu and Shiva are the Three Original Manifestations of Brahman, Sarasvati Lakshmi and Parvati/Kali are the Three Original Manifestations of Shakti, each empowering her husband with the Power he needs to carry out his part in the Cycle.<br /> <br /> Of course, Hinduism being the ridiculously branching and forking thing that it is, you will undoubtedly find sects and strains that disagree with parts of that, but it's a good point to start from nonetheless <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Incidentally, have I mentioned how much I love that Yoga is the Deva PSP now? Cuz I love it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1344,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","user":"Samudra","date":". 02-08-2017, 03:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T12:09:50-08:00","id":1045898,"title":"","content":"Hmm. Since discussing real-life religion is obviously not the topic of this thread, I'll go ahead and assume this is about storytelling.<br /> And in stories, what makes a divine being different from mortals is not always power (although it is an important attribute). Tales of gods failing at something, or having to work hard for their goals, are too widespread for that. <br /> I believe what we should be looking at is - the awe they inspire. The sheer metaphysical importance of these beings. Because, first and foremost, they are numinous. And they matter in the grand scheme of things.<br /> This does not mean they are unapproachable or alien. They still have personalities, strengths and weaknesses, they act - and it's not fake. But there is always more. There is always something transcendent hidden by the mist.<br /> When Inanna disappears, when Demeter misses her daughter, when Telipinu is in hiding - the world itself is diminished. When Indra killed Vritra - it was much more than a victory against a monster, or the end of a physical drought.<br /> The level on which gods operate is one we can touch only in moments of great inspiration. And, while a character in a story may forget, the storyteller should keep this in mind.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5530-xanthias","name":"Xanthias","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T12:54:11-08:00","id":1045922,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xanthias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045898#post1045898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm. Since discussing real-life religion is obviously not the topic of this thread, I'll go ahead and assume this is about storytelling.<br /> And in stories, what makes a divine being different from mortals is not always power (although it is an important attribute). Tales of gods failing at something, or having to work hard for their goals, are too widespread for that. <br /> I believe what we should be looking at is - the awe they inspire. The sheer metaphysical importance of these beings. Because, first and foremost, they are numinous. And they matter in the grand scheme of things.<br /> This does not mean they are unapproachable or alien. They still have personalities, strengths and weaknesses, they act - and it's not fake. But there is always more. There is always something transcendent hidden by the mist.<br /> When Inanna disappears, when Demeter misses her daughter, when Telipinu is in hiding - the world itself is diminished. When Indra killed Vritra - it was much more than a victory against a monster, or the end of a physical drought.<br /> The level on which gods operate is one we can touch only in moments of great inspiration. And, while a character in a story may forget, the storyteller should keep this in mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>I Hope Scion offers the possibility of someone reaching godhood if they manage do to something like this even if theyre not scions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T13:07:21-08:00","id":1045931,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xanthias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045898#post1045898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm. Since discussing real-life religion is obviously not the topic of this thread, I'll go ahead and assume this is about storytelling.<br /> And in stories, what makes a divine being different from mortals is not always power (although it is an important attribute). Tales of gods failing at something, or having to work hard for their goals, are too widespread for that. <br /> I believe what we should be looking at is - the awe they inspire. The sheer metaphysical importance of these beings. Because, first and foremost, they are numinous. And they matter in the grand scheme of things.<br /> This does not mean they are unapproachable or alien. They still have personalities, strengths and weaknesses, they act - and it's not fake. But there is always more. There is always something transcendent hidden by the mist.<br /> When Inanna disappears, when Demeter misses her daughter, when Telipinu is in hiding - the world itself is diminished. When Indra killed Vritra - it was much more than a victory against a monster, or the end of a physical drought.<br /> The level on which gods operate is one we can touch only in moments of great inspiration. And, while a character in a story may forget, the storyteller should keep this in mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>I Hope Scion offers the possibility of someone reaching godhood if they manage do to something like this even if theyre not scions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T14:08:49-08:00","id":1045989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">\u201cCall themselves?&quot; asked Yama. &quot;You are wrong, Sam, Godhood is more than a name. It is a condition of being. One does not achieve it merely by being immortal, for even the lowliest laborer in the fields may achieve continuity of existence. Is it then the conditioning of an Aspect? No. Any competent hypnotist can play games with the self-image. Is it the raising up of an Attribute? Of course not. I can design machines more powerful and more accurate than any faculty a man may cultivate. Being a god is the quality of being able to be yourself to such an extent that your passions correspond with the forces of the universe, so that those who look upon you know this without hearing your name spoken. Some ancient poet said that the world is full of echoes and correspondences. Another wrote a long poem of an inferno, wherein each man suffered a torture which coincided in nature with those forces which had ruled his life. Being a god is being able to recognize within one's self these things that are important, and then to strike the single note that brings them into alignment with everything else that exists. Then, beyond morals or logic or esthetics, one is wind or fire, the sea, the mountains, rain, the sun or the stars, the flight of an arrow, the end of a day, the clasp of love. One rules through one's ruling passions. Those who look upon gods then say, without even knowing their names, 'He is Fire. She is Dance. He is Destruction. She is Love.' So, to reply to your statement, they do not call themselves gods. Everyone else does, though, everyone who beholds them.&quot;</span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>From <i>Lord of Light.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines.</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/user?u=451515\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Vance's Patreon</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance","name":"Robert Vance","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T15:46:19-08:00","id":1046035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Robert Vance</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045989#post1045989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From <i>Lord of Light.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Didnt read that one yet, but yeah it does answer the question","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-09T02:06:47-08:00","id":1046218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045922#post1045922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I Hope Scion offers the possibility of someone reaching godhood if they manage do to something like this even if theyre not scions</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bI think that varies by pantheon, but I believe there was a mention somewhere of the Shen recognizing Taoist immortals. I'm pretty sure they come from mortal origins.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12689-creepyshutin","name":"CreepyShutIn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12689_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-09T10:29:47-08:00","id":1046376,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CreepyShutIn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1046218#post1046218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bI think that varies by pantheon, but I believe there was a mention somewhere of the Shen recognizing Taoist immortals. I'm pretty sure they come from mortal origins.</div> </div> </div> </div>true. my understanding is that reaching godhood really varries from pantheon from pantheon. The shen can elevate mortals to shenhood. But i don't think a 100% mortal person could become a theoi","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T11:22:59-08:00","id":1047328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1045931#post1045931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I Hope Scion offers the possibility of someone reaching godhood if they manage do to something like this even if theyre not scions</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> There's plenty of evidence to suggest they would / should be able to. Hell, the Voodoo pantheon seems to be based around ancestral spirits. So who's to say a powerful mortal can't inherit the station of godhood in the afterlife?<br /> <br /> <br /> And there are lots of other pantheons such as the Greeks, Hindu's and Chinese that specifically talk about an elixer of immortality in some fashion or another (Ambrosia, golden apples, Amrita, est). It could be offered to mortals who prove themselves worthy enough to receive its blessing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 02-11-2017, 02:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T13:02:46-08:00","id":1047363,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CreepyShutIn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1046218#post1046218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bI think that varies by pantheon, but I believe there was a mention somewhere of the Shen recognizing Taoist immortals. I'm pretty sure they come from mortal origins.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047328#post1047328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's plenty of evidence to suggest they would / should be able to. Hell, the Voodoo pantheon seems to be based around ancestral spirits. So who's to say a powerful mortal can't inherit the station of godhood in the afterlife?<br /> <br /> And there are lots of other pantheons such as the Greeks, Hindu's and Chinese that specifically talk about an elixer of immortality in some fashion or another (Ambrosia, golden apples, Amrita, est). It could be offered to mortals who prove themselves worthy enough to receive its blessing.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Immortals of Daoism (<i>xian</i>), who reach that state through alchemical elixiers or spiritual exercise, are not identical to gods (<i>shen</i>), though. The Chinese gods are still mostly ascended mortals, however, since Chinese religion (like Japanese religion as well) follows a model of temple worship automatically making a being/thing divine, no matter the origin. So, basically, both <i>xian </i>and <i>shen</i> come (mostly) from mortals, but they are otherwise entirely different classes of beings. Watcher and I once discussed options for expressing these kinds of differences, but we don't have a solid model yet.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T15:36:40-08:00","id":1047424,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047328#post1047328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And there are lots of other pantheons such as the Greeks, Hindu's and Chinese that specifically talk about an elixer of immortality in some fashion or another (Ambrosia, golden apples, Amrita, est). It could be offered to mortals who prove themselves worthy enough to receive its blessing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Amrita, similarly, technically grants Immortality, not necessarily Divinity. It's kinda vague as to what it does, since it only really appears in one myth, the Churning of the Ocean, in which the sage Durvasa is angered by Indra's disrespect and curses him and all his court of Vedic deities to lose their immortality. The Trimurti advise Churning the Primordial Ocean to retrieve Amrita which will restore Immortality to them, and after many shenanigans including teeming up with the Asura and Vishnu variously manifesting his Kurma (Turtle) and Mohini (Enchantress) Avatara, while also finding the time to get married to Lakshmi, succeed.<br /> <br /> There are a few Indian folk deities that are deified mortals, but these are rare and/or extremely local. By and large any deities that are born mortal and became Gods later in life are considered Avatara of one God or another...whether these are later interpretations tacked on to once purely mortal origin deities, or if that was always the case is usually difficult or impossible to determine at this point.<br /> <br /> Mortals DO become gods in the Vedas...the most famous of which are the Ribhus, a trio of brothers who achieved Divinity by their mastery of Craftsmanship...specifically, they are said to have created the ladles and other implements, out of the single existing implement of the God Tvastar, used during the Yagya (fire sacrifice) that is the cornerstone of Vedic religion. The Ribhus even have a few hymns dedicated to them in the Rig Veda. Ila, daughter of Manu is similarly deified for having laid down the orginal rules of sacrifice (this is probably the same Ila who in later Hinduism is reimagined as the genderfluid ancestress of the Lunar Dynasty).<br /> <br /> With the rise of the Trimurti and Tridevi and the considerable lessening of importance of most of the Vedic deities, the idea of mortals becoming Gods gets mostly phased out...now mortals strive to become Sages in service to the two Triads (or whichever member of such or their extended family you personally prefer)...and Trimurti focused Hindu myths and scripture tend to regularly show off said Sages overpowering the Vedic era Gods (such as the time the Shiva worshiping Durvasa stripped the Gods of their immortality (see above), or the time the Sarasvati worshiping Vishvamitra created a new Svarga with it's own set of Gods because Indra annoyed him). Hell, whereas the Rig Veda only ever mentions three Worlds...Bhur, the Earth where mortals live, Svar, Heaven where Indra and the other Deva dwell, and Bhuvar, Space, which is basically the wind and firmament and empty space in between Heaven and Earth, later Puranic Cosmology adds three entire Realms on top of Svarga for Sages in service to the Trimurti and Tridevi to find a place in. Even better, at the end of every set of Four Yugas (a Manvantara), all the realms upto and including Svarga are destroyed...but not the ones above it, so while Indra and his court will die at the end of each Manvantara, the Sages in the higher realms will be fine and dandy.<br /> <br /> Ok, that was supposed to be a post about mortals becoming deities in Hinduism and somehow meandered off tobecome a post about how thoroughly modern day Trimurti and Tridevi focussed Hinduism tends to love trashing the Vedic deities. My apologies.<br /> <br /> Getting back on track...<br /> <br /> Speaking from a game perspective, most Sages in Hinduism are clearly (IMO) just Scions of the Trimurti and Tridevi with the Chosen Origin who have achieved God level Power and just don't self-identify as Gods. Hell, their 'choosing of disciples' might just be how they refer to Choosing Scions of their own.<br /> <br /> Tl;dr : Amrita makes you immortal, not necessarily Divine. Most exceptional Mortals in Hinduism are likely to get Chosen by the Gods as Scions instead of being deified outright. Do not piss off a Sage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T15:58:10-08:00","id":1047434,"title":"","content":"I didn't understand half of that, mostly due to name dropping a bunch of stuff from a foreign language / religion. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> But from the way it was portrayed in 1st edition, Hinduism believes the physical world is an illusion and seeks to transcend its earthly connections, as well as recognizes a system of constant death and rebirth. So who's to say the occasional lucky mortal doesn't achieve enlightenment at some point, and ascends to a status mortals can only describe as divinity?<br /> <br /> <br /> From my understanding, Buddha isn't actually considered a god, either, he's just an extremely powerful mortal who became one with the universe and then went on to humble deific figures throughout mythology.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T16:13:01-08:00","id":1047438,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047434#post1047434\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't understand half of that, mostly due to name dropping a bunch of stuff from a foreign language / religion. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> But from the way it was portrayed in 1st edition, Hinduism believes the physical world is an illusion and seeks to transcend its earthly connections, as well as recognizes a system of constant death and rebirth. So who's to say the occasional lucky mortal doesn't achieve enlightenment at some point, and ascends to a status mortals can only describe as divinity?<br /> <br /> <br /> From my understanding, Buddha isn't actually considered a god, either, he's just an extremely powerful mortal who became one with the universe and then went on to humble deific figures throughout mythology.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what I'm saying, what defines divinity is really Hart to point outt","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T16:20:48-08:00","id":1047443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047434#post1047434\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't understand half of that, mostly due to name dropping a bunch of stuff from a foreign language / religion. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I apologize for that <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> If there's anything specific you would like a clarification on please feel free to ask or PM me. English is not my first language, so sorry if I defaulted to non-English words a bit too much in that explanation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047434#post1047434\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But from the way it was portrayed in 1st edition, Hinduism believes the physical world is an illusion and seeks to transcend its earthly connections, as well as recognizes a system of constant death and rebirth. So who's to say the occasional lucky mortal doesn't achieve enlightenment at some point, and ascends to a status mortals can only describe as divinity?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, this could totally happen <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I think you could probably model that with the mortal Magic system in Origin...from what little we've seen of it, practice of Yoga could probably be modeled with that, and Yoga is generally understood to be the source of Enlightenment in Hinduism. Most non-enlightened mortals would probably not know the difference. I'm just saying that Amrita isn't the way this is likely to happen. Most of the superpowererd Sages of myth I mentioned earlier are likely mortal Yoga practitioners who impressed the gods enough to be Chosen as Scions and ascend to God tier.<br /> <br /> As for the Illusory Nature of Reality, that is a whole different topic with a lot of interpretations about exactly what Illusion even means in the Hindu context, and would probably be a post unto itself, which I'm not derailing this thread with <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1344,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","user":"Samudra","date":". 02-11-2017, 07:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T18:15:54-08:00","id":1047468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047424#post1047424\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Getting back on track...<br /> <br /> Speaking from a game perspective, most Sages in Hinduism are clearly (IMO) just Scions of the Trimurti and Tridevi with the Chosen Origin who have achieved God level Power and just don't self-identify as Gods. Hell, their 'choosing of disciples' might just be how they refer to Choosing Scions of their own.<br /> <br /> Tl;dr : Amrita makes you immortal, not necessarily Divine. Most exceptional Mortals in Hinduism are likely to get Chosen by the Gods as Scions instead of being deified outright. Do not piss off a Sage.</div> </div> </div> </div>I found that to be a really interesting post, thanks so much for the explanation Samudra! I find the idea of the pre-Triumvi Deva a really interesting area to look at just for my personal curiosity since I adore being able to see such clear shifts over time in fundamental structure. That was really neat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047438#post1047438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's what I'm saying, what defines divinity is really Hart to point outt</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you mean, like, in terms of game mechanics Nicolas? I'm totally sorry if you originally meant that! I actually have a system to determine who is a God, Titan, Lesser God, etc for when I am writing up Pantheons if you would like to see that. I thought you meant, like, what each Pantheon in its real-world religious system defined as a God and the like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 02-11-2017, 09:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T04:35:05-08:00","id":1047651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1045353-what-it-takes-to-be-a-god?p=1047468#post1047468\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I found that to be a really interesting post, thanks so much for the explanation Samudra! I find the idea of the pre-Triumvi Deva a really interesting area to look at just for my personal curiosity since I adore being able to see such clear shifts over time in fundamental structure. That was really neat.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Do you mean, like, in terms of game mechanics Nicolas? I'm totally sorry if you originally meant that! I actually have a system to determine who is a God, Titan, Lesser God, etc for when I am writing up Pantheons if you would like to see that. I thought you meant, like, what each Pantheon in its real-world religious system defined as a God and the like.</div> </div> </div> </div>I totally meant in a religious way! But thank you for asking \ud83d\udc4d","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"t